Use cases must call the database (an outer concern), but cannot import it. Solved by **dependency inversion**: define the interface in the inner layer; implement in the outer layer.

```java
// === USE CASE LAYER ===
public interface OrderRepository {  // owned by use cases
    void save(Order order);
    Optional<Order> findById(String id);
}

public class PlaceOrderUseCase {
    private final OrderRepository orderRepo;

    public PlaceOrderUseCase(OrderRepository repo) { this.orderRepo = repo; }

    public Order place(String customerId, List<OrderItem> items) {
        Order order = Order.create(customerId, items);  // entity logic
        orderRepo.save(order);
        return order;
    }
}

// === INTERFACE ADAPTER LAYER ===
@Repository
class JpaOrderGateway implements OrderRepository {
    private final SpringDataOrderRepo jpa;
    public void save(Order o) { jpa.save(toJpaEntity(o)); }
    public Optional<Order> findById(String id) { return jpa.findById(id).map(this::toDomain); }
}
```

- The use case **declares** what it needs (`OrderRepository`).
- The adapter **implements** it (`JpaOrderGateway`).
- The dependency arrow: adapter → use case (inward). Runtime data flow: use case → adapter (via interface).
- Spring's `@Configuration` wires the implementation in at startup.

HTTP request enters; eventually reaches the use case.

```java
// === FRAMEWORKS LAYER ===
// Spring picks this up; framework concern only.

// === INTERFACE ADAPTER LAYER ===
@RestController
class OrderController {
    private final PlaceOrderUseCase placeOrder;

    @PostMapping("/api/orders")
    public OrderResponse create(@RequestBody @Valid CreateOrderRequest req) {
        Order order = placeOrder.place(req.customerId(), req.items());
        return OrderResponse.from(order);   // presenter logic
    }
}
```

- Controller belongs to interface adapters (it knows about Spring + use cases).
- Use case is called with **plain Java arguments** (no `HttpServletRequest`).
- Returns a domain object; presenter (`OrderResponse.from`) converts to HTTP shape.
- Use case has no idea HTTP exists.

```java
// === Input port (what callers see) ===
public interface PlaceOrderUseCase {
    PlaceOrderResponse place(PlaceOrderCommand cmd);
}

// === Input DTO (request) ===
public record PlaceOrderCommand(
    String customerId,
    List<OrderItemRequest> items,
    String idempotencyKey
) {}

// === Output DTO (response) ===
public record PlaceOrderResponse(
    String orderId,
    BigDecimal total,
    Instant placedAt
) {}

// === Output ports (what the use case needs) ===
public interface OrderRepository {
    void save(Order order);
    Optional<Order> findByIdempotencyKey(String key);
}

public interface InventoryGateway {
    void reserve(List<OrderItem> items);
}

// === Use case implementation ===
public class PlaceOrderInteractor implements PlaceOrderUseCase {
    private final OrderRepository orderRepo;
    private final InventoryGateway inventory;

    public PlaceOrderInteractor(OrderRepository repo, InventoryGateway inv) {
        this.orderRepo = repo;
        this.inventory = inv;
    }

    @Override
    public PlaceOrderResponse place(PlaceOrderCommand cmd) {
        // 1. Idempotency check
        var existing = orderRepo.findByIdempotencyKey(cmd.idempotencyKey());
        if (existing.isPresent()) {
            return toResponse(existing.get());
        }
        // 2. Domain logic via entities
        var items = cmd.items().stream().map(OrderItem::from).toList();
        inventory.reserve(items);
        var order = Order.create(cmd.customerId(), items, cmd.idempotencyKey());
        // 3. Persist
        orderRepo.save(order);
        return toResponse(order);
    }

    private PlaceOrderResponse toResponse(Order o) {
        return new PlaceOrderResponse(o.id(), o.total(), o.createdAt());
    }
}
```

Notice: zero Spring imports. The class is wired by `@Configuration` and called by an `@RestController` adapter.

**Input adapter (HTTP):**
```java
@RestController
class OrderController {
    private final PlaceOrderUseCase placeOrder;

    @PostMapping("/api/orders")
    public OrderResponse create(
            @Valid @RequestBody CreateOrderRequest req,
            @RequestHeader("Idempotency-Key") String key) {
        var cmd = new PlaceOrderCommand(req.customerId(), req.items(), key);
        var result = placeOrder.place(cmd);
        return OrderResponse.from(result);
    }
}
```

**Input adapter (Kafka consumer) — same use case:**
```java
@KafkaListener(topics = "order-commands")
public void onCommand(OrderCommandEvent evt) {
    var cmd = new PlaceOrderCommand(evt.customerId(), evt.items(), evt.requestId());
    placeOrder.place(cmd);
}
```

Same use case, two adapters. The use case doesn't care which one called it.

The use case is the natural transaction boundary: one user goal = one business transaction.

**Pattern A:** annotate the use case implementation in the adapter / config layer using AOP.
```java
@Configuration
class UseCaseConfig {
    @Bean
    public PlaceOrderUseCase placeOrder(OrderRepository r, InventoryGateway i) {
        return TransactionalProxy.wrap(new PlaceOrderInteractor(r, i));
    }
}
```

**Pattern B (pragmatic):** mark the implementation with `@Transactional`. Less pure (Spring annotation in the use case class), but commonly accepted.

**Pattern C (purist):** transaction is one of the gateways — `UnitOfWork` interface in the inner layer; Spring-backed implementation in the adapter. Most elegant but heavier.

Whichever pattern: the *boundary* is the use case, not the controller and not the repository.

Because dependencies are interfaces and the class is plain Java, testing is trivial:

```java
class PlaceOrderInteractorTest {
    @Test
    void places_order_when_inventory_available() {
        var fakeRepo = new InMemoryOrderRepository();
        var fakeInv = new InMemoryInventoryGateway();
        fakeInv.stock("sku-1", 10);

        var useCase = new PlaceOrderInteractor(fakeRepo, fakeInv);
        var cmd = new PlaceOrderCommand("cust-1", List.of(itemReq("sku-1", 2)), "key-1");

        var result = useCase.place(cmd);

        assertThat(result.orderId()).isNotBlank();
        assertThat(fakeRepo.findById(result.orderId())).isPresent();
        assertThat(fakeInv.remaining("sku-1")).isEqualTo(8);
    }

    @Test
    void returns_existing_order_for_duplicate_idempotency_key() {
        // ... idempotency test in 5 lines
    }
}
```

No Spring context. No DB. No Testcontainers. Tests run in milliseconds.

**Spring annotations are allowed only in `adapter` and `config`:**
- `@RestController`, `@KafkaListener` in `adapter/in/`.
- `@Repository`, `@Component` in `adapter/out/`.
- `@Configuration`, `@Bean` in `config/`.

**`domain` and `application` are pure Java:**
- Entities are records or POJOs; no `@Entity` (JPA mapping lives in `adapter/out/persistence/`).
- Use cases are plain classes with constructor injection; no `@Service`.
- Ports are interfaces with no annotations.

**Wiring example:**
```java
@Configuration
class UseCaseConfig {
    @Bean
    public PlaceOrderUseCase placeOrderUseCase(
            OrderRepository orderRepo,
            InventoryGateway inventory) {
        return new PlaceOrderInteractor(orderRepo, inventory);
    }
}
```

The interactor is plain Java; Spring sees the `@Bean` factory and wires the implementation it finds for each port.

Domain stays pure; JPA lives in the persistence adapter.

```java
// domain (no JPA)
public record Order(String id, String customerId, List<OrderItem> items, BigDecimal total) {
    public static Order create(String custId, List<OrderItem> items) { ... }
}

// adapter/out/persistence (JPA-flavored)
@Entity
@Table(name = "orders")
class OrderJpaEntity {
    @Id String id;
    String customerId;
    @OneToMany(cascade = CascadeType.ALL) List<OrderItemJpaEntity> items;
    BigDecimal total;
    // getters/setters
}

interface OrderSpringRepo extends JpaRepository<OrderJpaEntity, String> {}

@Repository
class JpaOrderRepository implements OrderRepository {
    private final OrderSpringRepo jpa;
    private final OrderMapper mapper;

    public void save(Order o) { jpa.save(mapper.toJpa(o)); }
    public Optional<Order> findById(String id) {
        return jpa.findById(id).map(mapper::toDomain);
    }
}
```

The domain `Order` and `OrderJpaEntity` are separate types with a mapper between them. Schema changes in JPA don't touch the domain.

For single-module projects, ArchUnit gives you compile-checked boundaries:

```java
class ArchitectureTest {
    private final JavaClasses classes = new ClassFileImporter()
        .importPackages("com.example.orders");

    @Test
    void domain_should_not_depend_on_anything() {
        noClasses().that().resideInAPackage("..domain..")
            .should().dependOnClassesThat().resideInAnyPackage(
                "..application..", "..adapter..", "..config..",
                "org.springframework..", "javax.persistence..")
            .check(classes);
    }

    @Test
    void application_should_only_depend_on_domain() {
        noClasses().that().resideInAPackage("..application..")
            .should().dependOnClassesThat().resideInAnyPackage(
                "..adapter..", "..config..", "org.springframework..")
            .check(classes);
    }

    @Test
    void controllers_should_be_in_web_adapter() {
        classes().that().areAnnotatedWith(RestController.class)
            .should().resideInAPackage("..adapter.in.web..")
            .check(classes);
    }
}
```

Run in CI; broken architecture fails the build.

**Anemic Order:**
```java
public class Order {
    private String id;
    private String customerId;
    private List<OrderItem> items;
    private OrderStatus status;
    private BigDecimal total;
    // getters and setters for all fields
    public void setStatus(OrderStatus s) { this.status = s; }
    public void setTotal(BigDecimal t) { this.total = t; }
}

@Service
public class OrderService {
    public void cancel(Order o) {
        if (o.getStatus() == OrderStatus.SHIPPED)
            throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot cancel shipped order");
        o.setStatus(OrderStatus.CANCELLED);
        // emit event, refund, etc.
    }
    public BigDecimal recalculateTotal(Order o) {
        BigDecimal sum = o.getItems().stream()
            .map(i -> i.getPrice().multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(i.getQty())))
            .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);
        o.setTotal(sum);
        return sum;
    }
}
```
The `Order` object holds data; `OrderService` holds the logic. The order can be put into invalid state from anywhere (`order.setTotal(-50)`) because setters are exposed.

**Rich Order:**
```java
public class Order {
    private final String id;
    private final String customerId;
    private final List<OrderItem> items;
    private OrderStatus status;
    private BigDecimal total;

    private Order(String id, String customerId, List<OrderItem> items) {
        if (items.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Order needs items");
        this.id = id;
        this.customerId = customerId;
        this.items = List.copyOf(items);
        this.status = OrderStatus.PENDING;
        this.total = computeTotal(items);
    }

    public static Order create(String customerId, List<OrderItem> items) {
        return new Order(UUID.randomUUID().toString(), customerId, items);
    }

    public void cancel() {
        if (status == OrderStatus.SHIPPED)
            throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot cancel shipped order");
        this.status = OrderStatus.CANCELLED;
    }

    public void addItem(OrderItem item) {
        if (status != OrderStatus.PENDING)
            throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot modify order after submission");
        items.add(item);
        this.total = computeTotal(items);
    }

    private static BigDecimal computeTotal(List<OrderItem> items) {
        return items.stream()
            .map(i -> i.price().multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(i.qty())))
            .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);
    }

    // getters only, no setters
}
```
Invariants enforced in the constructor and operations. `Order.total` cannot be set to a wrong value because there's no setter — it's recomputed from items every time.

Common myth: 'JPA needs public setters; therefore my entities must be anemic.'

Reality:
- JPA needs **a way to set fields** — either field access (no setter needed) or `protected` setters.
- `protected` keeps callers from setting directly while letting JPA reflect.
- Records work too with Hibernate 6+ for non-mutable value objects.

```java
@Entity
public class Order {
    @Id private String id;
    private String customerId;
    @OneToMany(cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<OrderItem> items;
    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING) private OrderStatus status;
    private BigDecimal total;

    protected Order() { /* JPA */ }

    public static Order create(String customerId, List<OrderItem> items) { ... }

    public void cancel() { ... }   // public business method
    // no public setters
}
```

Even cleaner: separate domain model from JPA entity (Clean Architecture style) and avoid the conflict entirely.

**Tool:** plain JUnit 5 + AssertJ. No Spring, no Mockito, no DB.

**What to test:** invariants enforced by constructors, behavior of business methods, equality of value objects.

```java
class OrderTest {
    @Test
    void cannot_create_order_with_no_items() {
        assertThatThrownBy(() -> Order.create("cust-1", List.of()))
            .isInstanceOf(IllegalArgumentException.class)
            .hasMessage("Order needs items");
    }

    @Test
    void cancel_succeeds_for_pending_order() {
        var order = Order.create("cust-1", List.of(item("sku-1", 2)));
        order.cancel();
        assertThat(order.status()).isEqualTo(OrderStatus.CANCELLED);
    }

    @Test
    void cannot_cancel_shipped_order() {
        var order = Order.create("cust-1", List.of(item("sku-1", 2)));
        order.markShipped("track-123");
        assertThatThrownBy(order::cancel)
            .isInstanceOf(IllegalStateException.class);
    }
}
```

These tests run in microseconds and form the bulk of the test suite — they cover the most logic-dense code in the app.

**Tool:** plain JUnit + **in-memory implementations of ports** (preferred) or Mockito (acceptable).

**Why fakes over mocks:**
- Fakes (in-memory ports) behave like real adapters — they store and retrieve data, raise the same exceptions.
- Mockito stubs answer one call; fakes work across multiple calls and tests.
- Tests using fakes are more behavior-focused, less coupled to implementation.
- Mocking 8 methods on 3 dependencies = brittle test that breaks on every refactor.

```java
class InMemoryOrderRepository implements OrderRepository {
    private final Map<String, Order> store = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    public void save(Order o) { store.put(o.id(), o); }
    public Optional<Order> findById(String id) { return Optional.ofNullable(store.get(id)); }
    public Optional<Order> findByIdempotencyKey(String key) {
        return store.values().stream()
            .filter(o -> key.equals(o.idempotencyKey()))
            .findFirst();
    }
}

class PlaceOrderInteractorTest {
    private final InMemoryOrderRepository repo = new InMemoryOrderRepository();
    private final InMemoryInventoryGateway inventory = new InMemoryInventoryGateway();
    private final PlaceOrderInteractor useCase = new PlaceOrderInteractor(repo, inventory);

    @BeforeEach
    void seedInventory() { inventory.stock("sku-1", 10); }

    @Test
    void places_order_when_stock_available() {
        var cmd = new PlaceOrderCommand("cust-1", List.of(itemReq("sku-1", 2)), "key-1");
        var resp = useCase.place(cmd);
        assertThat(resp.orderId()).isNotBlank();
        assertThat(repo.findById(resp.orderId())).isPresent();
        assertThat(inventory.remaining("sku-1")).isEqualTo(8);
    }

    @Test
    void returns_existing_order_for_duplicate_idempotency_key() {
        var cmd = new PlaceOrderCommand("cust-1", List.of(itemReq("sku-1", 2)), "key-1");
        var first = useCase.place(cmd);
        var second = useCase.place(cmd);
        assertThat(second.orderId()).isEqualTo(first.orderId());
        assertThat(inventory.remaining("sku-1")).isEqualTo(8); // not double-decremented
    }
}
```

No Spring, no DB, no @SpringBootTest. 100 use case tests run in under a second.

**Tool:** Spring's `@WebMvcTest` slice. Loads only the web layer (controllers, exception handlers, validators); not the full app context.

**What to test:** request mapping, validation, JSON serialization, status codes, error handling. **Not** business logic — that's covered in use case tests.

```java
@WebMvcTest(OrderController.class)
class OrderControllerTest {
    @Autowired MockMvc mvc;
    @MockBean PlaceOrderUseCase placeOrder;

    @Test
    void returns_201_with_created_order() throws Exception {
        when(placeOrder.place(any()))
            .thenReturn(new PlaceOrderResponse("order-1", new BigDecimal("100.00"), Instant.parse("2025-01-01T00:00:00Z")));

        mvc.perform(post("/api/orders")
                .header("Idempotency-Key", "k-1")
                .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
                .content("""
                    { "customerId": "cust-1", "items": [{"sku":"sku-1","qty":2}] }
                """))
            .andExpect(status().isCreated())
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.orderId").value("order-1"));
    }

    @Test
    void returns_400_when_customer_id_missing() throws Exception {
        mvc.perform(post("/api/orders")
                .header("Idempotency-Key", "k-1")
                .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
                .content("""
                    { "items": [{"sku":"sku-1","qty":2}] }
                """))
            .andExpect(status().isBadRequest());
    }
}
```

Fast (1-2s for full slice), tests exactly what the web adapter is responsible for.

**Tool:** Testcontainers + real Postgres (or your actual DB). **Avoid H2** — it's a different SQL dialect; tests pass that fail in production.

**What to test:** mapper between domain and JPA entity, JPA repository queries, native queries, transaction behavior.

```java
@DataJpaTest
@Testcontainers
@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = AutoConfigureTestDatabase.Replace.NONE)
class JpaOrderRepositoryTest {
    @Container
    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres = new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:16-alpine");

    @DynamicPropertySource
    static void config(DynamicPropertyRegistry r) {
        r.add("spring.datasource.url", postgres::getJdbcUrl);
        r.add("spring.datasource.username", postgres::getUsername);
        r.add("spring.datasource.password", postgres::getPassword);
    }

    @Autowired OrderSpringRepo springRepo;
    JpaOrderRepository adapter;

    @BeforeEach
    void wire() { adapter = new JpaOrderRepository(springRepo, new OrderMapper()); }

    @Test
    void saves_and_retrieves_order_with_items() {
        var order = Order.create("cust-1", List.of(item("sku-1", 2)));
        adapter.save(order);
        var loaded = adapter.findById(order.id()).orElseThrow();
        assertThat(loaded.items()).hasSize(1);
        assertThat(loaded.items().get(0).sku()).isEqualTo("sku-1");
    }
}
```

Real Postgres catches dialect issues; the test asserts what your app actually does in production.

**Tool:** WireMock for HTTP gateways; embedded brokers for Kafka.

```java
@WireMockTest(httpPort = 8089)
class StripePaymentGatewayTest {
    @Test
    void charges_successfully() {
        stubFor(post("/v1/charges")
            .willReturn(okJson("""
                { "id": "ch_123", "status": "succeeded" }
            """)));

        var gateway = new StripePaymentGateway("http://localhost:8089", "sk_test");
        var result = gateway.charge("cust-1", new BigDecimal("100.00"), "USD");
        assertThat(result.transactionId()).isEqualTo("ch_123");
    }

    @Test
    void handles_card_declined() {
        stubFor(post("/v1/charges")
            .willReturn(jsonResponse("""
                { "error": { "type": "card_error", "code": "card_declined" } }
            """, 402)));

        var gateway = new StripePaymentGateway("http://localhost:8089", "sk_test");
        assertThatThrownBy(() -> gateway.charge("cust-1", new BigDecimal("100.00"), "USD"))
            .isInstanceOf(PaymentDeclinedException.class);
    }
}
```

For critical external services, also run **contract tests** (Pact, Spring Cloud Contract) to catch when their schema changes.

Sparingly — for end-to-end smoke tests of critical flows. Goals:
- Verify the wiring works (all beans resolve, profiles load, security config doesn't reject things).
- Verify a critical happy-path goes from HTTP → use case → DB → HTTP response.

```java
@SpringBootTest
@AutoConfigureMockMvc
@Testcontainers
class OrderEndToEndTest {
    // 1-2 critical-path tests, not a thousand variations
    @Test
    void place_order_end_to_end() throws Exception {
        mvc.perform(post("/api/orders")
                .header("Idempotency-Key", "k-1")
                .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
                .content("..."))
            .andExpect(status().isCreated());
    }
}
```

Keep this small — 5-10 tests, not 500. Slow tests = ignored tests.

**Layered:**
```java
@Service
class OrderService {
    @Autowired OrderRepository orderRepo;        // Spring Data JPA interface

    @Transactional
    public Order placeOrder(String customerId, List<OrderItem> items) {
        OrderEntity entity = new OrderEntity(customerId, items);  // JPA entity
        return orderRepo.save(entity);                            // returns JPA entity
    }
}
```
Domain coupled to JPA: `OrderEntity` is `@Entity`; repository is `JpaRepository`.

**Clean Architecture:**
```java
// in inner layer; no Spring, no JPA
public class PlaceOrderInteractor implements PlaceOrderUseCase {
    private final OrderRepository orderRepo;   // a port (plain interface)

    public PlaceOrderInteractor(OrderRepository repo) { this.orderRepo = repo; }

    public Order place(String customerId, List<OrderItem> items) {
        Order order = Order.create(customerId, items);  // plain Java entity
        orderRepo.save(order);
        return order;
    }
}

// in adapter layer; can be JPA, Mongo, anything
@Repository
class JpaOrderRepository implements OrderRepository {
    public void save(Order order) { /* convert + jpa.save */ }
}
```

Don't sprinkle `log.info` inside business logic. Use AOP to log entry/exit of use cases:

```java
@Aspect
@Component
class UseCaseLoggingAspect {
    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger("usecase");

    @Around("execution(* com.example.application.service..*.*(..))")
    public Object logUseCase(ProceedingJoinPoint pjp) throws Throwable {
        var name = pjp.getSignature().toShortString();
        var start = System.nanoTime();
        try {
            log.info("USECASE_START name={}", name);
            Object result = pjp.proceed();
            log.info("USECASE_END name={} duration_ms={}",
                name, (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000);
            return result;
        } catch (Exception ex) {
            log.error("USECASE_ERROR name={} error={}", name, ex.getMessage(), ex);
            throw ex;
        }
    }
}
```

- Use cases stay log-free — just business logic.
- Logging policy lives in one place; consistent across all use cases.
- Adapters can have their own logging for adapter-specific concerns (HTTP request/response, DB query timing).

**Inside the domain** — if logging an event is part of the business rule (e.g., audit), emit a domain event instead of calling a logger directly.

Same approach: instrument at the boundary.

```java
@Aspect
@Component
class UseCaseMetricsAspect {
    private final MeterRegistry meters;

    @Around("execution(* com.example.application.service..*.*(..))")
    public Object measureUseCase(ProceedingJoinPoint pjp) throws Throwable {
        var name = pjp.getSignature().toShortString();
        var sample = Timer.start(meters);
        try {
            Object result = pjp.proceed();
            sample.stop(Timer.builder("usecase").tag("name", name).tag("status", "ok").register(meters));
            return result;
        } catch (Exception ex) {
            sample.stop(Timer.builder("usecase").tag("name", name).tag("status", "error").register(meters));
            throw ex;
        }
    }
}
```

**Distributed tracing** (OpenTelemetry):
- Adapters create / propagate spans (HTTP middleware does it automatically; Kafka consumer extracts trace context from headers).
- Spring Cloud Sleuth / OpenTelemetry's auto-instrumentation handles JDBC, HTTP client spans.
- The use case is inside a span automatically; never imports OpenTelemetry.

Authentication (who are you?) is a **web-adapter concern**. Spring Security validates the JWT / session, builds a `Principal`.

```java
@Configuration
class SecurityConfig {
    @Bean
    SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .csrf(c -> c.disable())
            .authorizeHttpRequests(a -> a
                .requestMatchers("/api/public/**").permitAll()
                .anyRequest().authenticated())
            .oauth2ResourceServer(o -> o.jwt(Customizer.withDefaults()));
        return http.build();
    }
}
```

The controller extracts user ID from the Spring Security context and passes it to the use case as a plain string:
```java
@PostMapping("/api/orders")
public OrderResponse create(@AuthenticationPrincipal Jwt jwt, @RequestBody CreateOrderRequest req) {
    var customerId = jwt.getSubject();
    return placeOrder.place(new PlaceOrderCommand(customerId, req.items(), req.idempotencyKey()));
}
```

Use case sees only `customerId: String` — doesn't know about JWT, session, or Spring Security.

Two layers of authorization:

**Edge / endpoint-level (web adapter):**
- Coarse role-based: 'only admins can call /admin/*'.
- Spring Security `@PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")` on the controller.
- Stops unauthenticated / wrong-role requests at the door.

**Domain / fine-grained (entity / use case):**
- 'A user can cancel only their own order.'
- Belongs in the entity or use case as an explicit business rule.
```java
public void cancel(String requestingUserId) {
    if (!customerId.equals(requestingUserId)) {
        throw new AccessDeniedException("Cannot cancel another user's order");
    }
    if (status == OrderStatus.SHIPPED) throw new IllegalStateException(...);
    this.status = OrderStatus.CANCELLED;
}
```

**Why split:** edge security is policy (can this caller hit this URL?). Domain authorization is business rule (can this user perform this action on this object?). Mixing them in one annotation hides intent.

A user goal = a business transaction. The use case is the natural boundary.

**Pattern A (pragmatic, common):** `@Transactional` on the interactor implementation.
```java
@Component
public class PlaceOrderInteractor implements PlaceOrderUseCase {
    @Override
    @Transactional
    public Order place(PlaceOrderCommand cmd) { ... }
}
```
Mild Spring contamination, but it's only one annotation, in the implementation file (not in the port interface).

**Pattern B (purer):** wrap the interactor with a transactional proxy in `@Configuration`:
```java
@Bean
public PlaceOrderUseCase placeOrderUseCase(OrderRepository repo) {
    PlaceOrderUseCase impl = new PlaceOrderInteractor(repo);
    return TransactionInterceptor.proxy(impl);   // or AOP-based
}
```
Interactor stays Spring-free; transactional behavior added by the configuration.

**Pattern C (purist):** `UnitOfWork` interface in the inner layer; Spring-backed implementation in the adapter. Use case explicitly uses it: `unitOfWork.execute(() -> ...)`. Most elegant, heavier.

**Most teams pick Pattern A** for pragmatism. The boundary stays at the use case; that's the architectural goal.

`JpaRepository<OrderEntity, String>` is a **Spring Data infrastructure interface**:
- Knows about JPA (`@Entity`).
- Returns JPA entities.
- Lifecycle is tied to the persistence context.
- Methods named `findByCustomerIdAndStatus(...)` are translated into JPQL.

It's a great productivity tool for the **persistence adapter** — not the domain repository. Treating it as the domain repository:

```java
// ❌ Anti-pattern
@Service
class OrderService {
    @Autowired JpaRepository<OrderEntity, String> orderRepo;  // JPA interface
}
```

leaks JPA into the use case. JPA entities (with lazy proxies, dirty checking, and `@Entity`) become domain objects — you've coupled business logic to the persistence framework.

In Clean Architecture, `JpaRepository` is hidden behind the adapter — a useful tool to implement the port, but never the port itself.

```java
// === application layer ===
public interface OrderRepository {
    void save(Order order);
    Optional<Order> findById(String id);
    Optional<Order> findByIdempotencyKey(String key);
    List<Order> findActiveByCustomerId(String customerId);
}

// === adapter/out/persistence ===
@Entity
@Table(name = "orders")
class OrderJpaEntity {
    @Id String id;
    String customerId;
    String idempotencyKey;
    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING) OrderStatus status;
    @OneToMany(cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    List<OrderItemJpaEntity> items;
    BigDecimal total;
    Instant createdAt;
    // protected setters / no-arg ctor for JPA
}

interface OrderSpringRepo extends JpaRepository<OrderJpaEntity, String> {
    Optional<OrderJpaEntity> findByIdempotencyKey(String key);
    List<OrderJpaEntity> findByCustomerIdAndStatusIn(String customerId, Set<OrderStatus> statuses);
}

@Repository
class JpaOrderRepository implements OrderRepository {
    private final OrderSpringRepo jpa;
    private final OrderMapper mapper;

    public void save(Order order) {
        OrderJpaEntity entity = mapper.toJpa(order);
        jpa.save(entity);
    }

    public Optional<Order> findById(String id) {
        return jpa.findById(id).map(mapper::toDomain);
    }

    public Optional<Order> findByIdempotencyKey(String key) {
        return jpa.findByIdempotencyKey(key).map(mapper::toDomain);
    }

    public List<Order> findActiveByCustomerId(String customerId) {
        return jpa.findByCustomerIdAndStatusIn(customerId,
            Set.of(OrderStatus.PENDING, OrderStatus.CONFIRMED))
            .stream().map(mapper::toDomain).toList();
    }
}
```

Use cases depend on `OrderRepository`. JPA is invisible above the adapter.

**Approach 1: Add finder methods to the repository.**
```java
List<Order> findActiveByCustomerId(String customerId);
List<Order> findOverdueByDueDate(LocalDate now);
```
Fine for a handful of queries. Doesn't scale to dozens of report-style queries.

**Approach 2: CQRS-style separate query interfaces.**
```java
public interface OrderQueryService {
    OrderSummaryDto findSummary(String orderId);
    Page<OrderListDto> search(OrderSearchCriteria criteria, Pageable page);
    OrderStatsDto monthlyStats(YearMonth month);
}
```
- Returns DTOs, not domain objects (queries don't need domain methods).
- Implementation can use SQL views, projections, or even hit a separate read model.
- Keeps the write-side `OrderRepository` focused on the aggregate's lifecycle.

For read-heavy systems with complex query needs, separating commands from queries (CQRS lite) is the practical choice. Don't try to express every query as a domain repository method.
